::PC_U03_L03_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L03_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.9
Question:
What is sin(pi/6)?
{
~A. sqrt(3)/2#This is cos(pi/6), not sin(pi/6).
~B. 1#This is not the exact sine value for pi/6.
=C. 1/2#Correct. pi/6 is 30 degrees, and sine is the shorter leg over the hypotenuse.
~D. 0#This is sin(0), not sin(pi/6).
}
::PC_U03_L03_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L03_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.9
Question:
What is tan(pi/4)?
{
~A. sqrt(2)/2#This is sin(pi/4) or cos(pi/4), not tangent.
=B. 1#Correct. At pi/4, sine and cosine are equal, so tangent is 1.
~C. sqrt(3)#This is tan(pi/3), not tan(pi/4).
~D. 0#This is not the tangent value for pi/4.
}
::PC_U03_L03_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U03_L03_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.9
Question:
What is cos(pi/3)?
{
=A. 1/2#Correct. pi/3 is 60 degrees, and cosine is 1/2.
~B. sqrt(3)/2#This is sin(pi/3), not cos(pi/3).
~C. 1#This is cos(0), not cos(pi/3).
~D. 0#This is cos(pi/2), not cos(pi/3).
}
::PC_U03_L03_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U03_L03_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.9
Question:
What is sec(pi/3)?
{
~A. 1/2#This is cos(pi/3), not sec(pi/3).
~B. sqrt(3)/2#This is sin(pi/3), not sec(pi/3).
~C. sqrt(3)#This is not the reciprocal of cos(pi/3).
=D. 2#Correct. secant is the reciprocal of cosine, and cos(pi/3)=1/2, so sec(pi/3)=2.
}
::PC_U03_L03_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U03_L03_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.9
Question:
Which triangle helps find exact values for pi/4?
{
~A. 30-60-90 triangle#A 30-60-90 triangle is used for pi/6 and pi/3.
=B. 45-45-90 triangle#Correct. pi/4 is 45 degrees, so a 45-45-90 triangle gives exact values.
~C. 3-4-5 triangle only#A 3-4-5 triangle does not give the standard pi/4 unit circle values.
~D. No triangle can be used#Special triangles are exactly how these values are developed.
}